Nodes.Text:
{n}Irabeth's next letter arrives with a cart number underlined twice.{/n}
{n}"The carter answered me. His load was turned back before it reached Drezen. Your supplier charged for goods received, and your officer detained the man for failing to deliver them. Ask the officer who turned him back."{/n}
{n}Below the account she has written: "You said you wanted my judgment. This is it. Tell me what happened when you used it."{/n}

{n}The quartermaster brings the officer and the supplier to the yard. The supplier gives the carter's name as proof of delivery; the officer says he turned that same cart back when a patrol reported cultists on the road.{/n}
{n}Irabeth's underlined number matches both accounts. The quartermaster opens the gate book. The load never passed through it.{/n}
{n}The supplier reaches for his invoice. You keep it on the table. The officer admits that the detention order was entered later, without his earlier instruction beside it. The carter has spent three days waiting in a guardroom for refusing an order he had actually obeyed.{/n}

{n}The carter walks out with his cart number and the cancellation in his hand. The quartermaster strikes the delivery charge, signs the correction, and leaves the false claim attached to it for the investigators.{/n}
{n}The supplier leaves under escort. The officer remains to explain why he concealed his own order from the clerk who wrote the detention.{/n}
{n}You send Irabeth the entire account, including that question. Her advice has produced a correction she can check, not a flattering report of how readily you agreed with her.{/n}

{n}The courier brings a short letter. Irabeth checked the cancellation with the carter; he has reached home.{/n}
{n}"The correction stands. Keep the original order where the next officer can find it. I will not come back to enforce it for you."{/n}
{n}The second paragraph has been crossed out and begun again.{/n}
{n}"I am coming through Drezen after the next courier. I would like an evening with you. No account this time. I have finished the book, and I have an argument about its forest which I would rather have in person."{/n}

{n}Irabeth leaves a travelling pack beside the chair. There is no uniform in it. She holds up the book, then puts it down without opening it.{/n}
"I used the forest as an excuse. A poor one. You noticed."
{n}She smiles, briefly, then looks at you straight.{/n}
"I wanted another evening. With you. That doesn't put me back under your command. I am still leaving in the morning."

"I asked her before I packed."
{n}Irabeth takes a folded note from the book and hands it over. Anevia has written across the margin in a much less patient hand.{/n}
"'Beth comes home. You get an evening, not her commission. And stop sending her puzzles when you mean invitations.'"
{n}Irabeth folds it again.{/n}
"She wants the next evening with me. So do I. Now I want your answer. An evening with Irabeth. Can you manage that?"

"No. You asked for my judgment and then asked me to abandon it at the door. Anevia is my wife. I am going home to her."
{n}She lifts the pack onto her knee.{/n}
"You can still have the evening I offered."

"No. I left Drezen with her. She has earned better than a lie about why I came back through it."
{n}She closes the book.{/n}
"Ask for what I offered. Or leave it."

"Good."
{n}She sets down the pack and comes round the chair. Her hand closes behind your neck; her kiss is firm, unhurried, and followed by a small, exasperated laugh.{/n}
"I spent half the road deciding how to ask. Come here. We have an evening to waste."

{n}In the morning she fastens the travelling pack. From the window comes the noise of the garrison assembling; she glances toward it, then shoulders the pack.{/n}
"They have an officer. I have a road."
{n}She gives you one more kiss at the door.{/n}
"Next time, send an invitation. I can still say no to it."
{n}She leaves for home. Nobody carries a new commission after her.{/n}
